Acute Renal Colic
Acute Urgent Care
Radiating unilateral flank pain, Extreme restlessness, Haematuria, Calculus on CT KUB

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-045
Reason for Visit: Sudden, agonizing pain in the side and back, causing severe restlessness and vomiting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient pacing the house in sheer agony, vomiting repeatedly, and experiencing sudden, excruciating flank pain radiating to the groin. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to provide immediate, potent parenteral analgesia that oral medications cannot achieve, and to rule out life-threatening mimics such as a leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm, avoiding an unnecessary and highly distressing wait in an emergency department waiting room.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: This pain hit my back like a sledgehammer and shoots right down into my groin, I can't find a single comfortable position!
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with the sudden onset of excruciating, sharp, unilateral right flank pain that began 2 hours ago. The pain radiates directly to the ipsilateral right groin and testicle. The patient exhibits severe restlessness, constantly pacing, sitting, and writhing on the floor, unable to find a comfortable position. The pain is accompanied by profound nausea and three episodes of recurrent vomiting. The patient notes dysuria and severe urinary urgency, and reports that their last void contained pink-tinged urine (macroscopic haematuria). There is noticeable pallor and diaphoresis associated with the severe pain spikes.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, pacing the room rapidly, groaning.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk; supportive partner present.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient is in sheer agony, completely unable to lie still, visibly sweating and flushed.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals an absence of peritonism, rebound tenderness, or abdominal rigidity, indicating an extra-peritoneal process. There is pronounced, unilateral right costovertebral angle tenderness on gentle percussion. Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia and hypertension, entirely secondary to the intense sympathetic pain response. Temperature is normal, ruling out an infected, obstructed kidney at this stage. Expected investigations in an outpatient or ED setting would include a non-contrast CT kidney-ureter-bladder (KUB) demonstrating a radio-opaque calculus within the right urinary tract with proximal hydroureter.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; suitable for conservative management once pain is controlled.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took oral ibuprofen 1 hour ago with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Renal Colic - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (Leaking) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's young age, lack of a pulsatile expansile abdominal mass, and the classic radiation of pain to the groin accompanied by haematuria.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the extreme restlessness (appendicitis patients lie perfectly still), the lack of localized peritonitis in the right iliac fossa, and the presence of haematuria.

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absolute absence of a high fever or systemic chills, and the paroxysmal, colicky nature of the pain rather than a constant, dull ache.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s presentation is a textbook demonstration of the renal colic diagnostic tetrad: radiating unilateral flank pain, extreme restlessness, gross haematuria, and presumed calculus on imaging. The objective absence of fever or localized peritonitis confirms an uncomplicated, albeit excruciating, ureteric stone. The urgent after-hours visit was incredibly effective; by providing immediate, powerful intramuscular analgesia, the patient's severe distress was rapidly abolished, confirming the diagnosis and safely allowing for conservative home management, thereby sparing the patient a traumatic transfer and long wait in the Emergency Department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg for powerful ureteric smooth muscle relaxation/analgesia, and Ondansetron 4mg ODT for nausea. Pain reduced from 10/10 to 2/10 within 30 minutes. Prescribed a short course of oral NSAIDs and an alpha-blocker (Tamsulosin) to facilitate stone passage.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ensuring you drink at least 2.5 to 3 liters of water every single day, you constantly flush your kidneys and dilute the minerals in your urine, which is the most effective way to prevent these agonizing stones from forming in the first place.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urine strainer provided to catch the stone for laboratory analysis.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days for an outpatient non-contrast CT KUB.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you suddenly develop a high fever, uncontrollable shivering, or if the pain completely stops responding to the prescribed painkillers, you must proceed immediately to the Emergency Department, as this suggests the blocked kidney has become dangerously infected.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe pain is caused by a hard mineral stone scraping down the narrow tube connecting your kidney to your bladder.
Clinical Course: The injection has relaxed the tube temporarily. Most small stones will pass naturally within the next week. You will likely feel a sharp pinch when it finally enters the bladder, followed by complete relief.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Rest at home tonight. Use a hot water bottle on your lower back or groin to help soothe any residual cramping. Drink plenty of water to keep pressure behind the stone to push it out.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I gave you a strong anti-inflammatory injection because standard painkillers do not work well for the intense spasms of the kidney tube. I have also prescribed a daily tablet that relaxes the muscles of the lower urinary tract to help the stone pass more easily.
Actionable Steps: Strain your urine using the provided filter cone. If you catch the stone, bring it to your regular doctor so they can send it to the lab to see exactly what minerals it is made of, helping you adjust your diet properly.

LITE INFORMATION

Typical Renal Colic. Right flank/groin pain, macroscopic haematuria, extremely restless. Afebrile. Given IM Ketorolac and Ondansetron. Excellent response. Safe for home management.

LITE EDUCATION

Maintain high fluid intake. Take prescribed NSAIDs and Tamsulosin. Present to ED immediately if fever or rigors develop. Follow up with GP for CT KUB.
